            <ArticleTitle>Evaluation of negative emotional care experiences in burn care.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1923-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1365-2702.2008.02302.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM AND OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess recollection of negative emotional experiences during burn care.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Patients in intensive care frequently report negative emotional experiences. Patients with severe burns who are treated in intensive care units undergo painful care procedures, but there have been no recent evaluations of their care experiences.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Former burn patients (n = 42) were randomly assigned to three groups: postal questionnaire, telephone interview and face-to-face interview.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Assessments included negative care experiences (feelings of uncertainty, powerlessness, being afraid, insecure, being a nuisance, or neglected), severity of injury, patient satisfaction, personality traits and psychological symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Overall, the degree of recalled negative experiences was low and associated with greater severity of injury, more symptoms of post-traumatic stress disorder and lower satisfaction with care. The feeling of powerlessness was the most common, as 67% of participants had such feelings to some extent.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Overall, negative care experiences were uncommon and most prevalent among the severely injured. Such experiences were also associated with psychological symptoms and lower patient satisfaction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RELEVANCE TO CLINICAL PRACTICE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although relatively uncommon, negative emotional care experiences should be monitored more closely during care.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
